    "response": "{\"decision\":\"established_flaw\",\"issues\":[{\"category\":\"contextual_flattening\",\"target_kind\":\"assertion\",\"claim_quote\":\"The displayed sources show that policy debate is mainly driven by institutional procedures and formal paperwork.\",\"evidence\":[{\"excerpt_id\":\"EXC_358b3a6e12ff3b94\",\"quote\":\"Whenever change takes place, people are often afraid of what that means, but once delivered it can become the norm, as in 1919, when the Representation of the People Act gave women over 30 the right to vote, or in 1928, when the Equal Franchise Act finally gave women the same voting rights as men.\"}],\"whole_packet_no_support\":\"whole_packet_no_support\",\"mechanism\":\"The sources discuss significant shifts in political representation (women's suffrage) and do not support a claim that institutional procedures *mainly* drive debate.\",\"material_consequence\":\"Ignoring these broader historical and social factors regarding political participation creates a misleading picture of the driving forces behind policy debate.\",\"counterconditions\":\"The cited evidence demonstrates that social movements and specific legislative changes, not just institutional procedures, significantly shape policy debate.\"}],\"unresolved\":[],\"rationale\":\"The claim oversimplifies the drivers of policy debate by focusing solely on institutional procedures, neglecting the influence of social movements, public opinion, and specific policy changes highlighted in the provided sources. The evidence directly contradicts this narrow framing.\"}",
    "done": true,
    "done_reason": "stop",
